        "Before you can appreciate what a convolution buys you, you have to feel what it costs to go without one. The obvious way to feed an image to a neural network is to FLATTEN it - take the 2D grid of pixels, read it row by row into one long vector, and hand that vector to a standard fully-connected (dense) network. This works. On MNIST (28x28 grayscale digits) a plain MLP gets ~98% accuracy, which is genuinely good. So the lesson here is not 'MLPs can't do images' - they can, on small, centered, low-variation images. The lesson is WHY this approach falls apart the moment images get real, and what specific structural assumptions a CNN adds to fix it.",
